Welcome to the Apache HTTP server kit. This kit is designed to help understand how to query and get a start at analyzing logs generated by your Apache HTTP servers.

This playbook serves to get you oriented around the kit and provide some anchors into the data and dashboards.

# Integration Guide
An integration guide is available on [this documentation page](https://docs.gravwell.io/integrations/application/apache.html)

# Overview

There are 2 tags associated with this kit. The primary tag ($APACHE) contains access logs, and the error tag ($APACHE_ERR) captures errors from the web server. 

Configuration macros are used in the queries to easily swap in whichever tag you choose to use. 

- $APACHE - the integration guide assumes you use tag=apache on ingester, and have configured Apache for json output
- $APACHE_ERR - the integration guide assumes you use tag=apache-err

Queries cover the whole tag. To look at one site, filter on the vhost field (see below). To look at one server, filter on the [src field](https://docs.gravwell.io/search/src/src.html).

## Apache Connection Logs
The kit expects the JSON LogFormat from the [integration guide](https://docs.gravwell.io/integrations/application/apache.html). Connection logs contain the following fields:

```
tag=$APACHE ax
| table
```

Connection logs contain the following fields:

- time
- remote_addr
- method
- uri (the path only)
- query (the query string, including the leading ?)
- status
- bytes
- duration (microseconds)
- user_agent
- referer
- vhost
- forwarded_for (only if Apache is behind a reverse proxy; see Deployment modes)

The vhost field is the ServerName of the virtual host that served the request, so sites on the same server can be told apart. If your LogFormat doesn't have it, add a "vhost" field set to %v as the last field. Give every VirtualHost its own ServerName, or several sites log the same value. The Site Health and Requests By Site Over Time queries need this field and return nothing without it, and logs written before you add it have no vhost.

## Deployment modes
- **Web server:** everything in the kit works with the integration guide's LogFormat.
- **Behind a reverse proxy:** configure mod_remoteip as the guide describes. Otherwise every client IP in the kit is your proxy's address.
  - If you also log the X-Forwarded-For header as forwarded_for (the guide's Proxy Configuration section), the Apache - Direct Access Sources query lists clients that reached Apache without going through the proxy. Without that field it returns nothing.
  - The header is set by the client, so treat it as context, not proof.
- **Blank columns:** many detections show a forwarded_for column. It's blank unless you log the header behind a proxy, and shows the proxy chain when you do. Detections fire either way.
- **Not covered:** Apache acting as a reverse proxy to backends (no backend health or latency views), or as a forward proxy.

## Apache Error Logs
Error logs are assumed to be in Apache's default error log format. An autoextractor is configured for tag=apache-err, and can be accessed with the ax processor. This autoextractor must be updated if you use a different tag than apache-err.

```
tag=$APACHE_ERR ax
```

Error logs contain the following fields:

- timestamp
- module
- loglevel
- pid
- tid
- client_ip
- client_port
- error_code (the AH code, e.g. AH00124)
- message
- referer

tid and the fields after it are only present when Apache logs them for that event. For example, startup and shutdown messages have no client.

# Queries
Queries are broken out into 2 types and have appropriate labels applied for reference. 

## Stats
- Primarily used by dashboards to generate metrics and data that provides an overview of activity, though can be used for ad hoc searches in query studio. Operational queries (errors, response times, error log activity, per-site health) carry the operations label.

## Detections
- Queries built to detect very specific behavior that is indicative of malicious activity. These can be used ad hoc, serve as a basis for your own detection engineering efforts, or enabled as scheduled searches which can be used for alerts. Every detection ships with a disabled scheduled search.

# Dashboards
4 dashboards are provided in this kit. 

## Apache - Traffic Overview
High-level statistics of connections. Volume over time, top URI, user agent frequency, top client IPs, HTTP status code distribution, and bandwidth by client.

## Apache - Operations
Server health. Response time and server errors over time, error log levels over time, slowest URIs, server errors by URI, top error log messages, and a per-site (vhost) health table with request volume by site.

## Apache - Attack Surface
This dashboard may indicate suspicious activity. Queries are built with lists, and likely need to be extended and customized for your purposes, but these serve as a solid starting point.
- Provides information about errors. 
- A list of user agents are configured for suspicious UA. 
- A list of suspicious URIs that indicate probing behavior.

## Apache - IP Investigation
Prompts for an IP address, and will provide information about connection requests coming from it. Will surface user agents, errors, HTTP status codes, methods, and an activity timeline.

# Actionables
## Apache - IP Lookup
A variety of actions are provided for IP addresses in Apache connection logs. These allow you to quickly pivot to searching for specific activity associated to the requesting IP

Actions:
- IP Investigation Dashboard
- IP Activity timeline
- URI and Methods
- User Agents